---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { getCollection } from 'astro:content';
import { SITE } from '../config';

const posts = (await getCollection('blog', (p) => !p.data.draft))
  .sort((a, b) => b.data.date.valueOf() - a.data.date.valueOf())
  .slice(0, 3);
const fmt = (d: Date) => d.toLocaleDateString('en-GB', { year: 'numeric', month: 'short', day: 'numeric' });
---
<Base>
<section class="hero" style="border-top:0">
  <div class="wrap">
    <div class="hero-grid">
      <div>
        <span class="chip">Server hardening, cloud security, web app testing</span>
        <h1>Hardening systems. Testing defenses.</h1>
        <p class="lead">I'm Rao, a computer science graduate and cybersecurity practitioner. I help businesses secure their Linux servers, cloud infrastructure, and web applications through hands-on penetration testing and practical hardening, not checkbox audits.</p>
        <div class="actions">
          <a class="btn primary" href="/#contact">Start a conversation</a>
          <a class="btn" href="/#services">See what I do</a>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div aria-hidden="true">
        <div class="term">
          <div class="term-bar"><i></i><i></i><i></i><span>security-check</span></div>
          <div class="term-body">
            <div class="cmd"><b>$</b> harden web-server-01</div>
            <div class="ok">✓ Password logins turned off</div>
            <div class="ok">✓ Firewall rules in place</div>
            <div class="ok">✓ Automatic updates enabled</div>
            <div class="warn">! 2 settings still to tighten</div>
            <div class="dim">Hardening report ready, in plain English.</div>
            <div class="cmd"><b>$</b> <span class="cursor"></span></div>
          </div>
        </div>
        <p class="term-note">Illustration of a hardening summary.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <ul class="trust">
      <li><strong>Written scope first</strong>Nothing is changed or tested without your clear, written go-ahead.</li>
      <li><strong>Plain-English reports</strong>Findings ranked by what matters most, with steps to fix them.</li>
      <li><strong>Confidential by default</strong>What I find stays between you and me.</li>
      <li><strong>Fixes rechecked</strong>I confirm the problems you fix are really closed.</li>
    </ul>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="world" id="world">
  <div class="wrap">
    <div class="section-head">
      <h2>The internet never sleeps</h2>
      <p>Automated scanners knock on every public server, all day long. Most are harmless noise, but some aren't. Here's a simulated view of what that traffic looks like. My job is making sure your doors are locked.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="map"><canvas id="map" role="img" aria-label="Animated dotted world map showing simulated internet scanning traffic being blocked"></canvas></div>
    <div class="wgrid">
      <div class="count"><span class="dim">Probes blocked in this demo</span><strong id="n">0</strong><span class="meta">Simulated for illustration, not a real-time feed.</span></div>
      <ul class="feed" id="feed" aria-hidden="true"></ul>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section id="services">
  <div class="wrap">
    <div class="section-head">
      <h2>What I do</h2>
      <p>Most security problems start with systems left on their default settings. I lock them down properly, then test them to prove it works.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="services">
      <article class="service">
        <h3>Linux server hardening</h3>
        <p>I lock down the servers your business runs on, so they stay private, patched and quietly doing their job.</p>
        <p class="fit"><strong>Good for:</strong> websites, apps and internal tools running on Linux.</p>
        <ul><li>Safer SSH and user access</li><li>Firewall and unneeded services reviewed</li><li>Updates, logging and basic monitoring in place</li></ul>
      </article>
      <article class="service">
        <h3>Cloud security hardening</h3>
        <p>I tighten your cloud setup so the right people have access and nothing is open that shouldn't be.</p>
        <p class="fit"><strong>Good for:</strong> teams who moved to the cloud quickly and want a second pair of eyes.</p>
        <ul><li>Permissions cleaned up: who can access what</li><li>Public storage and exposed services closed</li><li>Settings aligned with good practice</li></ul>
      </article>
      <article class="service">
        <h3>Web application penetration testing</h3>
        <p>I test your website or web app the way an attacker would, then show you what I found and how to fix it.</p>
        <p class="fit"><strong>Good for:</strong> online shops, customer portals, booking and login systems.</p>
        <ul><li>Hands-on testing against the OWASP Top 10</li><li>Clear report with evidence and fixes</li><li>Recheck once the fixes are in</li></ul>
      </article>
    </div>
    <p class="addon"><strong>Also available:</strong> small Python tools that automate routine security checks, so they run every time without anyone having to remember.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section id="process">
  <div class="wrap">
    <div class="section-head">
      <h2>How we'd work together</h2>
      <p>No surprises. You always know what I'm doing and why.</p>
    </div>
    <ol class="steps">
      <li><h3>Talk</h3><p>We agree on what is in scope, what is off limits, and when testing happens. All in writing.</p></li>
      <li><h3>Review</h3><p>I examine your servers, cloud setup or web app carefully and safely.</p></li>
      <li><h3>Report</h3><p>You get a plain-English report with findings ranked by how much they matter.</p></li>
      <li><h3>Fix and recheck</h3><p>I harden or help fix the important issues, then check that they are actually closed.</p></li>
    </ol>
  </div>
</section>

<section id="about">
  <div class="wrap about">
    <h2>About me</h2>
    <div>
      <p>I'm Rao, a computer science graduate focused on practical security. My work centres on Linux systems, cloud environments and web applications, and I write Python to automate the repetitive parts.</p>
      <p>I'm building my practice one carefully done project at a time, which means every client gets my full attention. I also write up what I learn on the blog, so you can see how I think before we ever talk.</p>
      <div class="principles">
        <div class="principle"><h3>I get permission first</h3><p>Every change and test has written authorisation and agreed boundaries.</p></div>
        <div class="principle"><h3>I explain, not impress</h3><p>You'll know what is wrong, why it matters and what to do about it.</p></div>
        <div class="principle"><h3>I'm honest about scope</h3><p>If a simpler fix is enough, I'll say so instead of selling you more.</p></div>
        <div class="principle"><h3>I respect your data</h3><p>Anything I see while working is kept confidential. I'm happy to sign an NDA.</p></div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section id="faq">
  <div class="wrap">
    <div class="section-head"><h2>Questions people ask</h2></div>
    <div class="faq">
      <details><summary>Will this disrupt my servers or website?</summary><p>I work to avoid it. Scope and timing are agreed in writing, changes are planned and tested carefully, and I keep a way to roll back. I'll ask before anything that could affect live services.</p></details>
      <details><summary>Do I need to be technical to work with you?</summary><p>No. I explain things in plain language. The report is written for the people making decisions, with the technical detail included for whoever does the fixing.</p></details>
      <details><summary>What will I receive at the end?</summary><p>A written report covering what I found, how serious each issue is, the evidence, and clear steps to fix it. I'm happy to walk you through it on a call.</p></details>
      <details><summary>How much does it cost?</summary><p>It depends on the size of what's being checked. Tell me about your setup and I'll give you a clear quote before any work starts.</p></details>
      <details><summary>Will my information stay private?</summary><p>Yes. Findings and access details stay confidential, and I'm happy to sign an NDA.</p></details>
      <details><summary>I'm not sure what I need. Where do I start?</summary><p>Send me a short message about what you run and what worries you. I'll tell you honestly what makes sense, even if that's less than you expected.</p></details>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section id="blog">
  <div class="wrap">
    <div class="section-head"><h2>From the blog</h2><p>Technical write-ups on Linux, cloud and web security.</p></div>
    <ul class="post-list">
{posts.map((p) => (
      <li><a href={`/blog/${p.id}/`}><span class="meta">{fmt(p.data.date)}</span><h3>{p.data.title}</h3><p>{p.data.description}</p></a></li>
    ))}
    </ul>
    <p class="more"><a href="/blog/">All posts</a></p>
  </div>
</section>

<section id="contact" class="contact">
  <div class="wrap">
    <h2>Tell me about your project</h2>
    <p>No pressure and no jargon. Tell me what you run and what worries you, and I'll reply with an honest view of what would help.</p>
    <ul class="include">
      <li>What you run: a website, servers, cloud accounts, or all three</li>
      <li>What's on your mind, or what prompted you to reach out</li>
      <li>Any deadlines I should know about</li>
    </ul>
    <div class="actions"><a class="btn primary" href={`mailto:${SITE.email}?subject=Security%20enquiry`}>{SITE.email}</a></div>
    <div class="socials"><a href={SITE.github} rel="noopener">GitHub</a><a href={SITE.x} rel="noopener">X / @Raosecops</a></div>
  </div>
</section>

<script is:inline src="/map.js"></script>
</Base>
